    {
      "node_id": "kg_doc_10",
      "node_type": "kg",
      "content": "One of the oldest depictions of civil disobedience is in Sophocles' play Antigone, in which Antigone, one of the daughters of former King of Thebes, Oedipus, defies Creon, the current King of Thebes, who is trying to stop her from giving her brother Polynices a proper burial. She gives a stirring speech in which she tells him that she must obey her conscience rather than human law. She is not at all afraid of the death he threatens her with (and eventually carries out), but she is afraid of how her conscience will smite her if she does not do this.",
      "depth": 0,
      "parent_id": null,
      "value": 0.9999999428562827,
      "temperature": 0.9999999428562827,
      "expanded": false,
